{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-86676" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $G$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-86676" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $G,$ then $G$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $G=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $G,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nG={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 17\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-86676" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "59e784e7d9e4e012a796856f8cab92078f7e25c11af7a1af50eeb1676151a3c7"} {"question" "q-86676" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-86676" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 19 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 17\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-17 & 30 \\\\ -9 & 16\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 9 \\\\ -3 & 17\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-86676" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 17\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-81210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-81210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $E,$ then $E$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $E=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $E,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nE={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}12 & 12 \\\\ 16 & 24\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-81210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "59e784e7d9e4e012a796856f8cab92078f7e25c11af7a1af50eeb1676151a3c7"} {"question" "q-81210" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-81210" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-81210" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-86680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-86680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $E,$ then $E$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $E=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $E,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nE={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -15 \\\\ 12 & -30\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-86680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "59e784e7d9e4e012a796856f8cab92078f7e25c11af7a1af50eeb1676151a3c7"} {"question" "q-86680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-86680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}12 & -19 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}19 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -21\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}19 & -12 \\\\ 21 & -32\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}9 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-86680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-37314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $F$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-37314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $F,$ then $F$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $F=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $F,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nF={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}].$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -10 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-26 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 28\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-37314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "59e784e7d9e4e012a796856f8cab92078f7e25c11af7a1af50eeb1676151a3c7"} {"question" "q-37314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-37314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-17 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 21\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-25 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 27\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-26 & -20 \\\\ 20 & 28\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-26 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 28\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-37314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-26 & -30 \\\\ 24 & 28\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-81215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $S$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-81215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $S,$ then $S$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $S=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $S,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nS={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & 1 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 & -12 \\\\ 10 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-81215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "59e784e7d9e4e012a796856f8cab92078f7e25c11af7a1af50eeb1676151a3c7"} {"question" "q-81215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-81215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}12 & -13 \\\\ 9 & 10\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}13 & -12 \\\\ 10 & -9\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}18 & 4 \\\\ 15 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-81215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}13 & -12 \\\\ 10 & -9\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-37601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${M}^{16},$ where\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[8]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[4]{2}\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/