4040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-37358" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${G}^{10},$ where\n\n$$\nG=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-37358" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $G:$\n\n$$\nG={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSince $G=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${G}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{G}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{G}^{10} & =P{D}^{10}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{10}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{2}^{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-2)^{10}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1024\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1024 & 0 \\\\ 1024 & 1024\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1024\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-37358" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "59e784e7d9e4e012a796856f8cab92078f7e25c11af7a1af50eeb1676151a3c7"} {"question" "q-37358" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-37358" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1024 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ -1024 & 1024\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ -2048 & 1024\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1024\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1024\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-37358" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1024 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1024\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-37257" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${A}^{101},$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-37257" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $A:$\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSince $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{101} & =P{D}^{101}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{101}(\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{1}^{101} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{101}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-37257" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "59e784e7d9e4e012a796856f8cab92078f7e25c11af7a1af50eeb1676151a3c7"} {"question" "q-37257" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-37257" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}100 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 100\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-37257" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-86694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]T=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-86694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $T:$\n\n$$\nT={{{[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $T$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-86694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "59e784e7d9e4e012a796856f8cab92078f7e25c11af7a1af50eeb1676151a3c7"} {"question" "q-86694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-86694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-86694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-86691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]E=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-86691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E:$\n\n$$\nE={{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-86691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Cours