\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-37363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $L:$\n\n$$\nL={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $L$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $L$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9c54d08b38c79e8edf19dcc8303effc8bfa777ffce8bd5457e4d3b82c0e0156"} {"question" "q-37363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "59e784e7d9e4e012a796856f8cab92078f7e25c11af7a1af50eeb1676151a3c7"} {"question" "q-37363" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differen